 # Geometry Bias Loss
 ## Overview
-Geometry Bias Loss is a regularization term used in differentiable volume rendering to enforce geometric consistency between the radiance field and an implicit Signed Distance Function (SDF) field [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of surface misalignment by penalizing the absolute SDF value at the volume-rendered surface point, ensuring that the radiance field's opacity distribution aligns with the zero-level set of the implicit geometry [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from sibling methods like SRDF-SDF Consistency Loss by focusing specifically on the alignment of the rendered surface point with the SDF field, rather than enforcing sign consistency across multiple views or field invariance [S1, 15302-srdf-sdf-consistency-loss.md, unofficial].
+Geometry Bias Loss is a regularization term used in differentiable volume rendering to enforce geometric consistency between the radiance field and an implicit Signed Distance Function (SDF) field [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of surface misalignment by penalizing the absolute SDF value at the volume-rendered surface point, ensuring that the radiance field's opacity distribution aligns with the zero-level set of the implicit geometry [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on [Implicit Field Consistency](17875-implicit-field-consistency.md) by enforcing that the volume-rendered surface point lies on the zero-level set of the implicit field [S1, unofficial].
 
